<!-- ================= HERO ================= -->
<header class="hero">
  <div class="wrap hero-grid">
    <div>
      <span class="kicker" data-scramble>Quantum mechanics · de Broglie · Bohr–Sommerfeld</span>
      <h1 class="hero-title">Standing <span class="thin">waves</span><br>in electron <em>orbitals</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">An electron bound to a nucleus does not orbit like a planet. It is a <strong>de&nbsp;Broglie wave</strong> that can only persist in one precise way — as a <strong>standing wave</strong> that closes on itself. Where those waves fit, atoms live. Where they don&rsquo;t fit, nothing is allowed.</p>
      <div class="hero-ctas">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#standing">Open the wave lab
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#shots">Jump to the quantum shots</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta">
        <div><b>Periodicity</b><span>2πn / λ</span></div>
        <div><b>Nodes allowed</b><span>n − 1</span></div>
        <div><b>Allowed states</b><span>Countable &amp; stable</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-visual" data-par="0.05">
      <canvas id="heroCanvas" class="orb-canvas" aria-label="Animated quantum shot of the 2p orbital with a circulating electron probability wave"></canvas>
      <div class="hero-caption"><span class="dot"></span> live render · ψ(r,&nbsp;θ,&nbsp;φ)² — 2p, n=2</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <a class="scroll-hint" href="#standing"><span>scroll</span><span class="chev"></span></a>
</header>

<!-- ================= 01 STANDING WAVE ================= -->
<section class="section" id="standing">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
      <span class="kicker">01 · The rule of closure</span>
      <h2>Orbits that <em>fit</em> — and the ones that don&rsquo;t</h2>
      <p class="lede">In 1913 Niels Bohr wrote a strange restriction on the hydrogen atom: an electron may orbit only at radii where an <strong>integer number of its wavelengths</strong> wraps exactly around the circle. De Broglie&rsquo;s 1924 insight turned that ad hoc rule into physics — the electron <em>is</em> a wave, and only standing waves are stable. Try it below.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lab">
      <div class="lab-side">
        <div class="panel" data-reveal>
          <h3>Wave controls <span class="tag">n = λ⁻¹ · 2π</span></h3>
          <div class="ctrl">
            <div class="ctrl-head"><label for="nSlider">Wavelength λ</label><span class="val" id="nVal">4λ around orbit</span></div>
            <input type="range" id="nSlider" min="1" max="9" step="1" value="4" aria-label="Number of wavelengths around the orbit">
          </div>
          <div class="ctrl">
            <div class="ctrl-head"><label for="ampSlider">Amplitude</label><span class="val" id="ampVal">60%</span></div>
            <input type="range" id="ampSlider" min="10" max="100" step="5" value="60" aria-label="Wave amplitude">
          </div>
          <div class="ctrl">
            <div class="ctrl-head"><label>Presets</label></div>
            <div class="chips" id="presetChips" role="group" aria-label="Wavelength presets">
              <button class="chip" data-n="1">1λ</button>
              <button class="chip" data-n="2">2λ</button>
              <button class="chip" data-n="4">4λ</button>
              <button class="chip" data-n="7">7λ</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="panel" data-reveal style="--d:.12s">
          <h3>Standing or not? <span class="tag">boundary test</span></h3>
          <div class="verdict" id="verdict" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-main">
        <div class="stage" data-reveal>
          <canvas id="labCanvas" aria-label="Interactive circle with a travelling wave; shows whether it forms a standing wave"></canvas>
          <div class="hud hud-tl" id="labHud">ψ(r,t) = A·e<sup>i(nθ−ωt)</sup></div>
          <div class="hud hud-tr" id="labHud2">traveling mode</div>
          <div class="hud hud-bl">drag λ until the wave closes on itself</div>
        </div>
        <p class="lab-note" data-reveal>
          <b>How to read it:</b> the wave travels around the ring. When <span class="eq">2π = nλ</span>, every crest lands on the crest it just left — the pattern freezes into a <b>standing wave</b> with <span class="eq">n</span> nodes and the orbit becomes a <em>Bohr orbit</em>. Change <span class="eq">λ</span> and watch interference cancel the pattern away. Drag the slider; the verdict updates live.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 02 QUANTUM SHOTS ================= -->
<section class="section sec-alt" id="shots">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
      <span class="kicker">02 · Quantum shots</span>
      <h2>Three waves, folded into <em>one</em> orbital</h2>
      <p class="lede">A full orbital is a product of three independent standing waves: one radial, one polar, one azimuthal. Each carries an integer — the <strong>quantum numbers</strong> <span class="eq">n, ℓ, m</span> — and each integer is simply a node count. The shapes below are the real, analytically computed hydrogen solutions, rendered as probability clouds.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="shot-grid">
      <article class="shot" data-reveal>
        <span class="num">i</span>
        <div class="shot-visual">
          <svg viewBox="-105 -105 210 210" role="img" aria-label="Radial standing wave: concentric rings with one node">
            <g fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" opacity=".9">
              <circle r="18"/><circle r="42"/><circle r="66" opacity=".7"/><circle r="90" opacity=".45"/><circle r="112" opacity=".25"/>
            </g>
            <g stroke="var(--hot)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 5" fill="none" opacity=".85"><circle r="30"/><circle r="54"/><circle r="78"/><circle r="101"/></g>
            <line x1="0" y1="-112" x2="0" y2="112" stroke="var(--line-strong)" stroke-width="1"/>
            <circle r="5" fill="var(--hot)"/>
            <text x="8" y="-88" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--faint)">r</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Radial wave <em style="color:var(--accent-2);font-style:italic">R<sub>nℓ</sub>(r)</em></h3>
        <p class="desc">The wave breathing in and out along the radius. It must vanish at the nucleus <span class="eq">R(0)=0</span> and fade to zero at infinity — a standing wave trapped in a box whose walls are the potential well. Its zeros are the <b>radial nodes</b>.</p>
        <div class="facts">
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Nodes</span><span class="v"><em>n − ℓ − 1</em></span></span>
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Quantum no.</span><span class="v">n, ℓ</span></span>
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Sets</span><span class="v">shell, size &amp; energy</span></span>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="shot" data-reveal style="--d:.1s">
        <span class="num">ii</span>
        <div class="shot-visual">
          <svg viewBox="-105 -105 210 210" role="img" aria-label="Polar standing wave: lobes above and below the equator">
            <g fill="var(--accent)" opacity=".14"><ellipse rx="92" ry="92"/><ellipse rx="60" ry="60"/></g>
            <g stroke="var(--accent-2)" fill="none" stroke-width="2">
              <path d="M 0 -96 C 34 -60 34 -14 10 0" opacity=".9"/><path d="M 0 -96 C -34 -60 -34 -14 -10 0" opacity=".9"/>
              <path d="M 0 96 C 34 60 34 14 10 0" opacity=".55"/><path d="M 0 96 C -34 60 -34 14 -10 0" opacity=".55"/>
            </g>
            <line x1="-96" y1="0" x2="96" y2="0" stroke="var(--hot)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 5" opacity=".85"/>
            <line x1="0" y1="-96" x2="0" y2="96" stroke="var(--line-strong)" stroke-width="1"/>
            <text x="10" y="-60" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--faint)">θ</text>
            <text x="70" y="-8" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--hot)">node</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Polar wave <em style="color:var(--accent-2);font-style:italic">Y(θ)</em></h3>
        <p class="desc">The wave standing between pole and equator. Associated Legendre functions <span class="eq">P<sub>ℓ</sub><sup>m</sup>(cosθ)</span> are standing waves on a half-sphere: exactly <span class="eq">ℓ − |m|</span> nodal rings where the amplitude hits zero, and the rest is lobes.</p>
        <div class="facts">
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Nodes</span><span class="v"><em>ℓ − |m|</em> (rings)</span></span>
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Quantum no.</span><span class="v">ℓ, m</span></span>
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Sets</span><span class="v">tilt &amp; lobes</span></span>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="shot" data-reveal style="--d:.2s">
        <span class="num">iii</span>
        <div class="shot-visual">
          <svg viewBox="-105 -105 210 210" role="img" aria-label="Azimuthal standing wave: a ring with two opposite lobes">
            <circle r="88" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-strong)" stroke-width="1"/>
            <path d="M 0 0 C 22 -34 74 -34 88 -6 A 88 88 0 0 1 88 6 C 74 34 22 34 0 0" fill="var(--accent)" opacity=".75"/>
            <path d="M 0 0 C -22 -34 -74 -34 -88 -6 A 88 88 0 0 0 -88 6 C -74 34 -22 34 0 0" fill="var(--hot)" opacity=".65"/>
            <line x1="0" y1="-88" x2="0" y2="88" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>
            <circle r="4" fill="var(--hot)"/>
            <text x="14" y="-44" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--accent)">+e<sup>i mφ</sup></text>
            <text x="-78" y="52" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--hot)">−e<sup>i mφ</sup></text>
            <text x="4" y="-92" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="var(--faint)">node</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Azimuthal wave <em style="color:var(--accent-2);font-style:italic">e<sup>i m φ</sup></em></h3>
        <p class="desc">The wave winding around the azimuth. To return to its starting value after one full turn, <span class="eq">e<sup>i m 2π</sup></span> must equal 1 — so <span class="eq">m</span> is an integer. It is literally the standing-wave condition from §01, now on the φ circle. Each step of <span class="eq">m</span> adds a nodal plane through the axis.</p>
        <div class="facts">
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Nodes</span><span class="v"><em>2|m|</em> (planes)</span></span>
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Quantum no.</span><span class="v">m</span></span>
          <span class="fact"><span class="k">Sets</span><span class="v">orientation &amp; L<sub>z</sub> = mℏ</span></span>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 03 INTERACTIVE ORBITAL LAB ================= -->
<section class="section" id="lab2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
      <span class="kicker">03 · Interactive lab</span>
      <h2>Pick a quantum shot. <em>Rotate it.</em></h2>
      <p class="lede">Every card below is a genuine hydrogen eigenfunction — the spherical harmonics evaluated point by point, with the radial factor for that shell. Drag to orbit the cloud; the shading is the probability density <span class="eq">|ψ|²</span>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qlab">
      <div class="panel" data-reveal>
        <h3>Quantum shot <span class="tag">n · ℓ · m</span></h3>
        <div class="shots" id="shotPicker" role="group" aria-label="Choose an orbital"></div>
        <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--faint); margin:1rem 0 0">
          <b style="color:var(--muted)">ℓ</b> = orbital angular momentum /2ℏ ·
          <b style="color:var(--muted)">m</b> = projection on z ·
          <b style="color:var(--muted)">nodes</b> total = n−1
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="qlab-view" data-reveal style="--d:.1s">
          <canvas id="orbCanvas" aria-label="Rotating 3D probability cloud of the selected orbital"></canvas>
          <div class="hud hud-l" id="orbLabel">ψ = ψ₁₀₀</div>
          <div class="hud hud-r" id="orbStats">nodes 0 · radial 0 · angles 0</div>
          <div class="hud hint">drag to rotate</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q-readout" data-reveal style="--d:.18s">
          <div><b>Principal n</b><span id="roN">1</span></div>
          <div><b>Angular ℓ</b><span id="roL">0</span></div>
          <div><b>Magnetic m</b><span id="roM">0</span></div>
          <div><b>Total nodes</b><span id="roNodes" class="accent">0</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 04 SHAPES ================= -->
<section class="section sec-alt" id="shapes">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
      <span class="kicker">04 · The shape library</span>
      <h2>Every shape is a <em>node count</em> in disguise</h2>
      <p class="lede">Chemists memorise these silhouettes; the wave picture lets you derive them. Each contour below is where the standing wave&rsquo;s amplitude reaches 55% of its maximum — the lobes are simply where the wave&rsquo;s energy lives.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="shapes" id="shapeGrid">
      <div class="shape" data-reveal>
        <svg viewBox="-60 -60 120 120"><circle r="46" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".85"/><circle r="46" fill="none" stroke="var(--plus)" stroke-width="1" opacity=".5"/><circle r="6" fill="var(--hot)"/></svg>
        <span class="tag">s · <em>ℓ=0</em></span><span class="mdeg">m = 0</span>
      </div>
      <div class="shape" data-reveal style="--d:.05s">
        <svg viewBox="-60 -60 120 120"><ellipse cx="-24" cy="0" rx="26" ry="15" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".85" transform="rotate(45 -24 0)"/><ellipse cx="24" cy="0" rx="26" ry="15" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".8" transform="rotate(45 24 0)"/><line x1="-17" y1="-17" x2="17" y2="17" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/></svg>
        <span class="tag">p · <em>ℓ=1</em></span><span class="mdeg">m = 0</span>
      </div>
      <div class="shape" data-reveal style="--d:.1s">
        <svg viewBox="-60 -60 120 120"><ellipse cx="-24" cy="0" rx="26" ry="15" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".85" transform="rotate(-45 -24 0)"/><ellipse cx="24" cy="0" rx="26" ry="15" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".8" transform="rotate(-45 24 0)"/><line x1="17" y1="-17" x2="-17" y2="17" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/></svg>
        <span class="tag">p · <em>ℓ=1</em></span><span class="mdeg">m = ±1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="shape" data-reveal style="--d:.15s">
        <svg viewBox="-60 -60 120 120"><ellipse cx="-30" cy="-22" rx="19" ry="11" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".85" transform="rotate(-30 -30 -22)"/><ellipse cx="18" cy="-26" rx="19" ry="11" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".8" transform="rotate(30 18 -26)"/><ellipse cx="32" cy="16" rx="19" ry="11" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".7" transform="rotate(30 32 16)"/><ellipse cx="-16" cy="22" rx="19" ry="11" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".7" transform="rotate(-30 -16 22)"/></svg>
        <span class="tag">d · <em>ℓ=2</em></span><span class="mdeg">m = ±2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="shape" data-reveal style="--d:.2s">
        <svg viewBox="-60 -60 120 120"><ellipse cx="-30" cy="0" rx="22" ry="12" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".85" transform="rotate(90 -30 0)"/><ellipse cx="30" cy="0" rx="22" ry="12" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".8" transform="rotate(90 30 0)"/><ellipse cx="0" cy="-34" rx="22" ry="12" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".65"/><ellipse cx="0" cy="34" rx="22" ry="12" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".65"/></svg>
        <span class="tag">d · <em>ℓ=2</em></span><span class="mdeg">m = 0</span>
      </div>
      <div class="shape" data-reveal style="--d:.25s">
        <svg viewBox="-60 -60 120 120"><ellipse cx="-28" cy="-28" rx="15" ry="9" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".85" transform="rotate(45 -28 -28)"/><ellipse cx="12" cy="-32" rx="15" ry="9" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".8" transform="rotate(-20 12 -32)"/><ellipse cx="34" cy="-4" rx="15" ry="9" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".75" transform="rotate(30 34 -4)"/><ellipse cx="20" cy="26" rx="15" ry="9" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".7" transform="rotate(60 20 26)"/><ellipse cx="-14" cy="34" rx="15" ry="9" fill="var(--plus)" opacity=".7" transform="rotate(20 -14 34)"/><ellipse cx="-34" cy="10" rx="15" ry="9" fill="var(--minus)" opacity=".7" transform="rotate(-50 -34 10)"/></svg>
        <span class="tag">f · <em>ℓ=3</em></span><span class="mdeg">m = ±1</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 05 NUMBERS ================= -->
<section class="section" id="numbers">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
      <span class="kicker">05 · The standing wave, quantified</span>
      <h2>Integers, nodes &amp; <em>energy</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="stat" data-reveal>
        <div class="big">n − 1 <small>nodes</small></div>
        <div class="lab2">Per orbital</div>
        <p>Every orbital carries exactly <b>n&nbsp;−&nbsp;1</b> standing-wave nodes: (n−ℓ−1) radial + ℓ angular. Count the nodes and you know the shell.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-reveal style="--d:.08s">
        <div class="big">E<sub>n</sub> ∝ 1/n²</div>
        <div class="lab2">Hydrogen energy</div>
        <p>Each extra half-wave costs energy: E<sub>n</sub> = −13.6&nbsp;eV / n². The wave picture explains why the spectrum is <b>discrete</b>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-reveal style="--d:.16s">
        <div class="big">2ℓ + 1</div>
        <div class="lab2">m states per ℓ</div>
        <p>For a given angular momentum there are exactly 2ℓ+1 allowed azimuthal windings, from m = −ℓ to +ℓ. ℓ = 2 gives five d-shapes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-reveal style="--d:.24s">
        <div class="big">ℏ <small>per unit m</small></div>
        <div class="lab2">Angular momentum</div>
        <p>The azimuthal standing wave quantises spin about the axis: L<sub>z</sub> = mℏ, and the full magnitude |L| = √(ℓ(ℓ+1))&thinsp;ℏ.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 06 HISTORY ================= -->
<section class="section sec-alt" id="history">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
      <span class="kicker">06 · How the idea arrived</span>
      <h2>From postulate to <em>wave</em> in eleven years</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tl">
      <div class="tl-item" data-reveal>
        <span class="tl-year">1913</span>
        <h3>Bohr&rsquo;s quantum condition</h3>
        <p>Niels Bohr demands that only orbits carrying an integer of angular momentum — <b>mvr = nℏ</b> — are allowed. It works for hydrogen&rsquo;s spectrum, but the rule is a <b>postulate</b>: a boundary condition without a wave.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item" data-reveal style="--d:.06s">
        <span class="tl-year">1923</span>
        <h3>de Broglie: the electron is a wave</h3>
        <p>Louis de Broglie proposes that every particle carries a wavelength, <b>λ = h/p</b>. Matter itself, it turns out, can interfere.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item" data-reveal style="--d:.12s">
        <span class="tl-year">1924</span>
        <h3>Orbits explained as standing waves</h3>
        <p>Bohr&rsquo;s rule falls out immediately: a circular electron orbit of circumference <b>2πr</b> persists only if an integer number of de Broglie wavelengths closes the loop — <b>2πr = nλ</b>. A wave that fails to close on itself interferes with its own past and cancels. Allowed orbits are simply <b>standing waves</b>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item" data-reveal style="--d:.18s">
        <span class="tl-year">1926</span>
        <h3>Schrödinger&rsquo;s standing-wave equation</h3>
        <p>Erwin Schrödinger writes the wave equation for bound matter. Its stationary solutions — <b>standing waves in three dimensions</b> — are the orbitals, with n, ℓ, m emerging as node counts and boundary conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item" data-reveal style="--d:.24s">
        <span class="tl-year">1960</span>
        <h3>Electron diffraction seals it</h3>
        <p>K. J. Thomson &amp; A. Rees obtain the first clear <b>electron diffraction patterns</b>, showing electrons forming interference fringes exactly like light — direct, visible proof of the matter wave.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

